# #1371: a nested type's symbol was invisible to the emitter, so an implicitly-abstract member of a
# NESTED @abstract class emitted a silently-throwing concrete member instead of an abstract one.
#
# NameResolver defines a nested type's symbol inside the enclosing class's scope, so codegen's global
# lookup returned null and `_currentTypeSymbol` was null for EVERY nested type. The read sites had
# been patched with a decorator-only fallback, which drops the implicit-stub arm — hence the throwing
# concrete member. The fix makes the symbol reachable through the enclosing symbol's NestedTypes, so
# the read sites are pure symbol reads again.
#
# The pin is BEHAVIOURAL, not just a snapshot: `Concrete` overrides the nested abstract member, and a
# C# class cannot override a non-abstract, non-virtual property — so if the member regresses to
# concrete this fixture stops compiling rather than silently printing the same thing.


@abstract
class Outer:
    @abstract
    class Inner:
        property get value(self) -> int:
            ...

    class Concrete(Inner):
        @override
        property get value(self) -> int:
            return 42


def main() -> None:
    c = Outer.Concrete()
    print(c.value)
